  },
  {
   "wiki": "en",
   "page": "List of Ecuadorian flags",
   "section": "Historical flags",
   "file": "Flag of Ecuador (1845–1860).svg",
   "text": "! scope=\"row\" | | 1845–1860 | The Cuenca Convention ratified, by decree of 6 November 1845, a change to a deeper blue, and the increase in the number of stars to seven \"as symbols of the seven provinces which make up the Republic\".",
   "kind": "table"
  },
  {
   "wiki": "en",
   "page": "List of Ecuadorian flags",
   "section": "Historical flags",
   "file": "Civil flag and ensign of Ecuador.svg",
   "text": "! scope=\"row\" | | 1860–1900 | Gabriel García Moreno, upon assuming power two days after the Battle of Guayaquil, reinstated the tricolor flag of Greater Colombia on 26 September 1860. In 1900, the flag was made the definitive national standard, and the coat of arms was added for official state use. The plain flag was established as the merchant ensign in 1900.",
   "kind": "table"
  },
  {
   "wiki": "en",
   "page": "List of Ecuadorian flags",
   "section": "Historical flags",
   "file": "Flag of Ecuador (1900–2009).svg",
   "text": "! scope=\"row\" | | 1900–2009 | The flag from 31 October 1900. In 1900, the flag was made the definitive national standard, and the coat of arms was added for official state use. The plain flag was established as the merchant ensign in 1900 a modification in November 2009. |}",
   "kind": "table"
  },
